---
import Base from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Install and set up"
  description="Install postiee, create your own LinkedIn app, store credentials, and log in. Ten minutes the first time."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Getting started</p>
  <h1>Install and set up</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    Ten minutes the first time, because you have to create your own LinkedIn
    app. Every step after that is one command.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Install</h2>

  <p>
    The installers detect your OS and CPU, prefer a per-user location so you
    don't need admin rights, and warn you if the install directory isn't on your
    <code>PATH</code>.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    label="macOS · Linux"
    code="curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/aswathas/postiee/main/install.sh | sh"
  />
  <CodeBlock
    label="Windows · PowerShell"
    code="irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/aswathas/postiee/main/install.ps1 | iex"
  />

  <h3>From source</h3>

  <p>Needs Go 1.26.9 or newer. The <code>sudo mv</code> is the only step that
  needs admin rights; build it anywhere you like.</p>

  <CodeBlock
    code={`git clone https://github.com/aswathas/postiee.git
cd postiee
go build -o postiee ./cmd/postiee
sudo mv postiee /usr/local/bin/`}
  />

  <div class="note info">
    <p>
      <strong>No Go toolchain needed</strong> for the one-liners — those download
      a prebuilt binary for your platform.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>2. Create your LinkedIn app</h2>

  <p>
    There is no shared client ID. Every person needs their own app, because
    LinkedIn grants scopes per application. Both products below are required:
    one lets you post, the other lets postiee work out who you are.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      Go to
      <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/developers/apps">linkedin.com/developers/apps</a>
      and click <strong>Create app</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Products</strong> tab → add <strong>Share on LinkedIn</strong>.
      This grants <code>w_member_social</code>, which is what writes the post.
      Self-serve, no review.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Products</strong> tab → add <strong>Sign In with LinkedIn</strong>.
      This grants <code>openid</code> and <code>profile</code>. Without it
      postiee cannot resolve your own member ID, and every post fails.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Auth</strong> tab → add this exact redirect URL:
    </li>
  </ol>

  <pre><code>http://127.0.0.1:8085/callback</code></pre>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Copy that exactly, including <code>127.0.0.1</code>.</strong>
      LinkedIn compares redirect URLs as exact strings, and
      <code>localhost</code> is not the same value to it as
      <code>127.0.0.1</code>. Registering <code>localhost</code> is the single
      most common reason login fails with
      <em>"redirect_uri does not match the registered value"</em>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you already registered a different value, set
      <code>POSTIEE_REDIRECT_URI</code> to match it instead.
      <code>auth login</code> prints the exact URI it is sending before it opens
      your browser, so you can compare the two directly.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Back on the <strong>Settings</strong> tab you'll see a <strong>Client ID</strong>
    and <strong>Client secret</strong>. You need both.
  </p>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Never paste either value into a chat, an issue, or a commit.</strong>
      The secret is a password for your app. The next step puts them in a local
      file that nothing else can read.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>3. Store your credentials</h2>

  <p>
    Write them to a file rather than passing them as flags — a flag value lands
    in your shell history and in process listings.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    code={`mkdir -p ~/.config/postiee
chmod 700 ~/.config/postiee

cat > ~/.config/postiee/credentials.toml <<'EOF'
client_id = "your_client_id"
client_secret = "your_client_secret"
EOF

chmod 600 ~/.config/postiee/credentials.toml`}
  />

  <p>
    On Windows the same file lives at
    <code>%USERPROFILE%\.config\postiee\credentials.toml</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Log in</h2>

  <CodeBlock code="postiee auth login --port 8085" />

  <p>
    Your browser opens, you approve, and the token is stored. postiee prints the
    exact redirect URI it is sending before it opens the browser — compare it
    against your Auth tab if login fails. The window stays open 15 minutes,
    which is enough for a password, a 2FA prompt, and an email code.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Token life is 60 days.</strong> Programmatic refresh is reserved
      for LinkedIn partners, so after expiry you re-run <code>auth login</code>.
      If you're still signed in to LinkedIn, the consent screen is skipped.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>5. Check it works</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    code={`postiee doctor
postiee auth status`}
  />

  <p>
    <code>doctor</code> reports where it found your config and credentials,
    whether a token is loaded, and whether <code>api.linkedin.com</code> is
    reachable. <code>auth status</code> confirms the token in one line.
  </p>

  <p>Then make the post that proves it:</p>

  <CodeBlock code='postiee post "first post via postiee" -v CONNECTIONS' />

  <div class="note info">
    <p>
      It prints a permalink and the post is on your feed. Nothing on LinkedIn
      can show you that record later — from here on,
      <code>postiee history</code> is how you find it. That is the whole reason
      this tool exists.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Where your files live</h2>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">What</th>
          <th scope="col">macOS / Linux</th>
          <th scope="col">Windows</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>App credentials — you write these</td>
          <td><code>~/.config/postiee/credentials.toml</code></td>
          <td><code>%USERPROFILE%\.config\postiee\credentials.toml</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Access token — postiee writes this after login</td>
          <td><code>~/.local/share/postiee/</code></td>
          <td><code>%USERPROFILE%\.local\share\postiee\</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>History database</td>
          <td><code>~/.config/postiee/history.db</code></td>
          <td><code>%USERPROFILE%\.config\postiee\history.db</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    <code>postiee doctor</code> prints the resolved path of each one, so you
    never have to guess.
  </p>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Back up <code>history.db</code>.</strong> It is the only surviving
      record of your posts — see <a href="/postiee/about">why</a>. Copying one
      file is the whole backup.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>If something goes wrong</h2>

  <p>
    Run <code>postiee doctor</code> first; it names what it found and what it
    couldn't. Then the
    <a href="/postiee/docs/commands#troubleshooting">troubleshooting table</a>
    maps LinkedIn's error text to a fix.
  </p>

  <h2>Next</h2>

  <p>
    <a href="/postiee/docs/commands">Commands</a> — every command and flag, or
    the <a href="/postiee/docs/limits">limits page</a> to find out what this
    can't do before you rely on it.
  </p>
</Base>